<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berlin Wall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Wall was a guarded barrier built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> from 13 August 1961 to cut off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a> and stop East Germans fleeing to the West. It became the leading symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its opening on 9 November 1989 accelerated the collapse of communist regimes across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a> in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-wall.jpg" alt="Berlin Wall" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlinermauer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>West Germany joined NATO in 1955, faced the <b>Berlin Wall</b> from 1961 and eased tensions with the East through Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik in the late 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From August 1989 East Germans occupied the West German embassy in Prague, and after 3 November thousands left Prague by train for the West; on 9 November the <b>Berlin Wall</b> fell.</blockquote><a href="/movement/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>East Germany's Auferstanden aus Ruinen spoke of a single German fatherland, which sat badly with the state after it built the <b>Berlin Wall</b> in 1961, so that on official occasions only its tune was played.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>These themes soon sat badly with the Cold War, above all after the East German government built the <b>Berlin Wall</b> in 1961.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a></li><li><blockquote>Overnight, East German forces closed the crossings and began building barriers that became the <b>Berlin Wall</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Speaking at the Brandenburg Gate, President Reagan challenged the Soviet leader to open the <b>Berlin Wall</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
